  "faq1Q": "What is Free Time?",
  "faq1A": "Free Time is the opposite of an app that wants your attention. It gives you points for the time your phone lies untouched. The further away the phone is, the more points. And you can compete with friends over who leaves it alone the most.",
  "faq2Q": "Why does it exist?",
  "faq2A": "Because the phone has become a guest that never goes home. It sits on the table at dinner, it comes to bed, it interrupts conversations, and it takes the small pauses where your mind would otherwise wander. Free Time is an attempt to turn that around: to make presence the thing that gets rewarded, and to make the screen a little less invasive in everyday life. Not with bans, but with a small, friendly score.",
  "faq3Q": "How does it count?",
  "faq3A": "On Android a small counter runs in the background all the time. When the screen locks or turns off, a session starts. When you unlock, it ends. No tapping required.\n\nOn iPhone, Apple does not allow that kind of background measurement. There you tap \"Put the phone down\" when you put it down and \"Stop\" when you pick it up. The app then checks whether the phone actually lay still and whether it was used in the meantime.",
  "faq4Q": "How are points awarded?",
  "faq4A": "You get 1 point per minute the phone lies there. That is multiplied by how \"gone\" it is:\n\n•  Sound on: 1×\n•  Vibrate: 1.5×\n•  Silent: 2×\n•  Do Not Disturb / Focus: 2.5×\n•  Airplane mode: 3× with Wi-Fi on, 4× with Bluetooth on, 5× with all radios off\n•  Turned off: 10×\n•  Restart: 50 points on top\n\nIf the phone lies with the screen on (but locked), points are halved. If you carry it around in a pocket, they are halved too. Multipliers stack, so silent in a pocket is 2 × 0.5 = 1×.\n\nExample: an hour on silent on the table is 120 points. An hour in airplane mode with all radios off is 300. A night turned off is about 4,800.",
  "faq5Q": "What is the daily goal?",
  "faq5A": "The daily goal is yours. The default is 600 points, which equals five hours on silent or two hours in airplane mode. You can change it under \"More\". The goal only affects the ring on the front page, not the standings among friends.",
  "faq6Q": "Can you cheat?",
  "faq6A": "Yes, if you want to. You can leave the phone at home and go to work. But then you actually were without your phone, and that is the whole point. The app uses the phone's sensors to see whether it lay still and whether the screen was used, and gives fewer points when it cannot confirm it. But this is an app between friends, not a court.",
  "faq7Q": "Why is iPhone different from Android?",
  "faq7A": "Apple does not let apps read the silent switch, airplane mode, or whether the phone is locked, and apps may not run in the background for hours. So on iPhone you start and stop sessions yourself and tell the app whether it was on silent or in airplane mode. Android gives access to all of it, so there it is measured automatically.",
  "faq8Q": "What data does the app store?",
  "faq8A": "Only what is needed: when a session started and ended, which state the phone was in (sound, silent, airplane, off), whether it lay still, and whether the screen was on. The app does not see which apps you use, who you message, or where you are. Your friends can see your weekly points and total time without phone, not the individual sessions.",
  "flagUnverified": "unverified",
  "measureTitle": "How we measure",
  "measureIntro": "On iPhone you start and stop a session yourself. Apple does not let apps read the silent switch, airplane mode or the lock screen, so that part is yours to tell us. Two optional permissions make the rest more precise.",
  "measureSessionTitle": "You start and stop",
  "measureSessionBody": "Tap \"Put the phone down\" when you put it down and \"Stop\" when you pick it up. That is the whole basis for your points.",
  "measureFocusTitle": "Focus",
  "measureFocusBody": "Do Not Disturb gives 2.5× points. iOS only tells the app a single yes/no: is a Focus on. Not which one, not who is messaging you.",
  "measureScreenTimeTitle": "Screen Time",
  "measureScreenTimeBody": "Makes your sessions verified. iOS notifies the app if the phone is used for more than a minute during a session. The app never sees which apps or for how long. Apple's own prompt mentions restricting apps – Free Time does not use that.",
  "measurePrivacy": "Everything is read on the phone and stays there. The only thing sent to the server is a session's start, end, mode, number of pickups, and whether it was verified.",
